{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Old and satisfied.</b> Since Abraham was blessed in every thing and was complete in all kinds of wealth and honor, Scripture now tells us that he departed from this world old and satisfied (שבע) from all good. And therefore it did not say 'satisfied (ושבע) days,' for that would have implied that he lacked other things and was satisfied only with the days of his life. Therefore Scripture informs us of his good qualities, that he departed from the world satisfied with every thing, for he did not crave luxuries. As it is written of David, peace be upon him (Psalms 21:3): ״תאות לבו נתתה לו״ — 'the desire of his heart You have given him.' And not like the rest of the people of the world, of whom it is written (Ecclesiastes 5:9): ״אוהב כסף לא ישבע כסף״ — 'he who loves silver will not be satisfied with silver.' And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: no person departs from the world with even half his desires in his hand. If he has in his hand one hundred of desire, he desires two hundred; if he has two hundred, he desires four hundred. As it is said: 'he who loves silver will not be satisfied with silver.'" "<b>And was gathered to his people.</b> Since death is the separation of the composite, and upon separation the honor of the soul is gathered to its people, therefore it says ויאסף, and this refers back to the honor (כבוד), for the soul is called 'honor' (כבוד) because it is hewn from the Throne of Glory (כסא הכבוד). And its root, to which it is destined to return, is the Throne of Glory. And this is (Psalms 30:13): ״למען יזמרך כבוד ולא ידום״ — 'so that glory may sing to You and not be silent.' And this is (Isaiah 58:8): ״כבוד ה' יאספך״ — 'the glory of HaShem shall gather you,' for the glory of HaShem is gathered there. And the expression 'his people' (עמיו) is mentioned in the same manner as is said of the soul (Exodus 31:14): ״מקרב עמיה״ — 'from among her people.' For the other souls that did not sin are its people and its family, and so too here. And the expression גויעה (expiring) is used of him, and גויעה applies only to the righteous. And they raised an objection in the Gemara: but the generation of the flood — גויעה is said of them, as it is written (Genesis 7:21): ״ויגוע כל בשר הרומש על הארץ״ — 'and all flesh that creeps upon the earth expired,' and it is written: ״כל אשר בארץ יגוע״ — 'all that is on the earth shall expire.' And the answer given: we speak of גויעה and gathering (אסיפה)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:03.756836914Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}